---
title: HTTP cancellation and WebSockets
description: Scope a request, exchange messages and host an echo route using the existing scheduler.
---

<span hidden data-pagefind-filter="SDK:preview.3 candidate"></span>

**Unreleased preview.3 candidate.** These APIs are absent from the published
preview.2 download. Its [HTTP reference](/LUI/docs/sdk-0.1.0-preview.2/api/services/httpservice/)
keeps the released contract. Candidate SDK packaging and native desktop journeys
have their own qualification gates; this page does not publish a new download.

Use `HttpService` for a request/response. Use WebSocket when both sides need to
send messages on one connection. Both use `task.spawn` and the existing LUI
scheduler; application code does not create threads or pump a message loop.

## Cancel one action

Create a token with `HttpService:CreateCancellationToken()`, pass it in request
options, and retain it with the action's Cancel button or owner. Cancel is
idempotent. A cancelled token stays cancelled; create another for another action.

```luau
local Http = app:GetService('HttpService')
local Token = Http:CreateCancellationToken()
task.spawn(function()
    local Ok, Response = pcall(function()
        return Http:RequestAsync({Url = 'https://example.com/', CancellationToken = Token})
    end)
    if Ok then print('[LUI:Example] HTTP status', Response.StatusCode)
    else print('[LUI:Example] Request failed or was cancelled') end
end)
-- From the action's Cancel callback: Token:Cancel()
```

This needs `network.client` and host permission for the destination. HTTPS validates
certificates; do not disable verification to make an example connect. The optional
JsonClient's `RequestAsync` accepts the same token. Cancellation affects only
requests or WebSocket work that intentionally shares the token. The existing
`CancelAll()` still cancels earlier HTTP requests runtime-wide; later requests
survive. Foreign tokens are refused and at most128 live tokens may be retained.

Expected network failures are catchable. HTTP error statuses are received
responses: inspect `Response.Success` and `StatusCode`. A failed connection,
timeout or cancellation throws instead. Do not treat all failures as invalid JSON
or print a private response body into framework diagnostics.

## Send and receive a real message

Copy `Examples/websocket-echo` from the prepared candidate SDK to a sibling project.
It has no optional dependencies. Use `lui check`, then:

```powershell
lui run --mode headless --grant network.client,network.server --http-origin http://127.0.0.1:18083 --diagnostics development
```

Port18083 must be free. This complete entry file runs both sides, verifies an actual
echo and closes the server. It does not report success from connection alone.

```luau
{{Example}}
```

`ConnectAsync`, `SendAsync` and `ReceiveAsync` yield; call them in scheduled tasks
or a hosted route handler, which is already scheduled. `network.client` permits
outbound connections; `network.server` permits hosted routes. Existing address and
port restrictions still apply. A WebSocket route shares its HTTP listener and is
registered before Start. Native upgrade validation completes before its handler
owns the socket. An optional Authorize callback must return true. It runs as a
scheduled hosted task and may yield on supported scheduler operations within the
shared request deadline. Failure refuses the upgrade without exposing callback
error text.

Received messages are readonly `{Data: string, Binary: boolean}` values. Strings
send text by default; buffers send binary. An optional Binary boolean overrides
that choice. Text must be valid UTF-8. Data received before your first receive
waits in a bounded inbox, in order. Only one receive can be outstanding per socket.

`Close()` starts a graceful handshake; accepted sends may finish within its
deadline. Continue receiving to observe an orderly close as nil. `Cancel()` aborts
immediately and is useful during owner teardown. Both are idempotent. State is
Connecting/Open/Closing/Closed. CloseCode and CloseReason describe the peer;
messages and reasons are untrusted application data.

## Defaults and optional controls

| Control | Default | Maximum |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ConnectTimeoutMs | 10,000ms | 60,000ms |
| CloseTimeoutMs | 5,000ms | 10,000ms |
| MaxMessageBytes / MaxFrameBytes | 1MiB each | Host ceilings, at most1MiB each |
| MaxQueuedBytes | 4MiB per direction | Host ceiling, at most4MiB |
| MaxQueuedMessages | 16 per direction | Host ceiling, at most16 |
| IdleTimeoutMs / PingIntervalMs | 0, disabled | 600,000ms |
| PongTimeoutMs | 10,000ms | 60,000ms |

Smaller options narrow the host's ceilings. Protocols selects offered subprotocols;
Headers and Origin supply application handshake data. CancellationToken scopes
connection work. Hosted options additionally accept Origins and Authorize. An
Origin-bearing request is rejected unless its Origin is explicitly listed;
originless native clients remain allowed. Origin policy is not authentication.
Use Authorize for application authorization.
There are eight retained socket handles per runtime, including closed handles
until released by the VM. Drop obsolete references; closing does not force GC.
Payload reservations are bounded separately from total OS/TLS or VM memory.

Saturated queues reject work; messages are not silently replaced. An inbox flood
closes the connection. A successful send means the complete frame was written,
not that a peer processed it. Outbound fragmentation, compression, automatic
reconnect, HTTP/2/3 upgrades and browser transports are outside this profile.
The app chooses retry, credentials, JSON schema and reconnection policy.

## Availability and deeper host controls

Windows/Linux native protocol, client/server, denial, cancellation, quotas and
headless journeys have integration tests. Desktop UI, physical input and a
packaged SDK recipient are distinct checks. Unsupported browser hosts refuse
these native services; raw backend objects never enter Luau.

Ordinary app authors use the services and declared grants above. Native embedders
may additionally narrow exact origins, resolved public addresses, concurrency and
reservation budgets through versioned `LuiHttp.h`/`LuiWebSocket.h` APIs. Those are
host/framework integration controls, not extra setup every Luau application needs.

